# Title: Grafted Into War
# Kind: episode
# Synopsis: Jon Murphy survives the infusion, grafts to an airship turret, witnesses a conscience-killing, falls when the airship is hit, and wakes as Myrmidon-009 with orders to find Dame Lyra of House Ophiuchus.

%% beat title:"The Infusion" chapter:"Grafted Into War" tone:dread pace:deliberate

Jon Murphy was strapped to an operating table in a room that smelled of antiseptic and heated copper. He had volunteered for the infusion because the alternative was a posting to the western front on foot, and he had seen what happened to the infantry on the western front.

The fluid entered through ports in both forearms simultaneously. It felt, at first, like hot metal flowing into his veins—coating the walls of his circulatory system in something that was neither liquid nor solid. Then it burned. Then the burning became something else entirely: a conductive presence that moved through him like a second nervous system installing itself without invitation.

He did not pass out. A voice from a speaker near the ceiling noted this with detached approval.

"Very good. You survived the infusion. That puts you in the upper twenty percentile. Congratulations."

He lay staring at the ceiling for a long time after that. His new blood moved through him differently than the old kind. Everything in the room had edges now—precise, legible edges. The single bulb above him. The thermostat on the wall. The two technicians on the other side of the glass.

A voice through the intercom: You should start to notice... three to four days. But we're already reading some minimal conductivity.

He noticed.

%% beat title:"The Training Arm" tone:alien pace:deliberate

A week later he was in a glass booth, manipulating a two-jointed robotic claw with his mind.

The sensation was surreal in the precise way that truly alien things are—not dreamlike, not hallucinatory, but matter-of-fact in a register that had no precedent. He could feel the claw as an extension of his own body. The ball joint at its terminus could rotate 260 degrees in any direction; past 180 degrees he felt the wrongness of it in his own shoulder, a phantom torque his body had no muscles to produce.

They called it the third arm in the briefings. He had a third arm under direct neural control, and it was simultaneously expansive and imprisoning—his sense of self now extended past his skin, but the extension was clumsy, and the clumsiness was his. Not the equipment's fault. His.

He practiced for six hours.

By the end he could pick up a cup without crushing it.

They said he was ready.

%% beat title:"Turret Thirty-Three" tone:tense pace:clipped

The world from inside Turret #33 was narrow and absolute.

Jon Murphy knew he was still a man—he could feel the distant weight of his own body somewhere above the hull, suspended in the infusion cradle, lungs still drawing air—but the body had become peripheral. What was real was the barrel: the heft of it, the cold drag of wind across the housing at altitude, the way target shapes resolved themselves in his expanded field of view not as sights but as decisions. Crosshairs existed inside his understanding now, not in front of his eyes.

Turret #34 was three meters to his right. He could feel it the way you feel a person standing close in a dark room.

He had come to recognize its habits. The small involuntary corrections when the airship banked. A slight hesitation before firing, barely a tenth of a second. #34's operator had a tell.

The engagement commenced. Below them, a mile down, target shapes resolved—uniformed, armed, moving.

He fired. He didn't think about it. That part of him had been worked smooth by training.

#34 was not firing.

"Number 34. You've stopped firing. Report."

He heard the command on the shared channel. He heard the response—a young voice, or what sounded like one, though he was beginning to understand that voice was just another thing the QE link carried.

"I'm sorry sir. I can't do this. They're people, I can't—"

He kept firing. He did not know what else to do.

"Ten seconds," said the command voice. "Or I'm cutting your QE link. Cowardice. War criminal. That's a death sentence, soldier."

Ten seconds passed the way irreversible things pass: all at once.

#34 went still. The barrel slumped a fraction—just the weight of the housing losing whatever animating intention had been holding it level. A machine in use looks different from a machine at rest, and #34 now looked like the latter.

The smell arrived two seconds later. Burnt ozone, acrid and specific, the particular sharpness of a thing that conducts no longer. It came in through whatever sensory bleed the QE link permitted, and Jon catalogued it without meaning to.

He kept firing.

%% beat title:"Thirty-Four" tone:horror pace:measured

Then #34 moved.

Not purposefully—not yet. The barrel swept a slow, patternless arc. No target. No tactical logic. It rotated a degree one direction, then back, then settled. The housing shivered. It swung again, a small correction, and then another, like a man rolling his shoulders in a chair before a long task.

Getting comfortable.

It opened fire without a word.

He watched the targeting patterns change. The new occupant of #34 was more aggressive than the last—no hesitation, no tenth-of-a-second tell. Whatever was inside the housing now, it had been here before.

Jon kept firing. He did not smell ozone. He considered, briefly, whether that made him better or worse than #34's first operator, then he set the question aside because it served no function, and he was a turret, and turrets did not ask questions.

He had learned that much.

%% beat title:"The Fall" tone:shock pace:fast

The RPG round came from the surface two seconds ahead of any warning the targeting sensors could give him.

He registered it at four hundred meters—a bright thermal signature, rising fast, correction trajectory locked to the aft starboard hull—and had begun to rotate before it hit.

The explosion was not loud in the way he expected. It was everywhere at once. A concussive inversion, as if the concept of direction ceased to apply, and then the airship was rolling and the horizon was wrong and turret #33's housing was tearing free of its mount in a shriek of metal he felt as his own ribs separating.

He fell.

A mile of sky.

Then nothing.

%% beat title:"The Spark" tone:existential pace:slow

The nothing was not nothing.

He had been here before—he knew this now, the way you know a place visited only in dreams. An infinite black sea. His memories were derelicts suspended in the dark: the smell of burnt ozone from #34, the drag of wind on a barrel he no longer had, the young voice saying they're people, I can't— in the last second before the link was cut.

He drifted. No direction. No instruction.

He was afraid, and then he was not, and then he was not anything at all.

Then something reached in to fish him out.

%% beat title:"M-009" tone:clinical pace:deliberate

Two legs. Two arms. A head.

He inventoried these with the focused attention of a man who has been other things and understands that this kind of accounting must be done carefully. The ground beneath his boots was soft. Moss. The sky above was gray. Wind moved across the housing of a weapon slung over his shoulder, and he recognized the sensation—wind on a housing—and understood that the word housing no longer meant what it had an hour ago.

A daemon deep within ran its inventory.

Myrmidon-009. This is you now.

He accepted this the way you accept a posting: without enthusiasm, without resistance.

His radio chirped.

"M-009. You've been grafted by standing order of K-13, Dame Lyra of House Ophiuchus. Locate and rendezvous with K-13 at the embedded coordinates. She's in the field. Over."

He stood still for a moment.

Somewhere in the derelict dark at the back of his mind, a memory was already dissolving—altitude, the drag of wind on a housing that no longer existed, #34 wriggling in its mount before it opened fire without a word. The logic of it evaporated like a dream. What remained was the smell of ozone and an unexamined conviction that turrets were not the worst thing you could be.

"M-009. Copy that. Over."

He checked the weapon, found the action sound, and moved out.
